Borderline personality disorder
Mental Health & Substance Use
Extreme fear of abandonment, Unstable interpersonal relationships, Recurrent self-harm or suicidality

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MHS-014
Reason for Visit: Acute crisis involving superficial self-laceration and severe emotional dysregulation following a perceived rejection.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and roommate
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute episode of self-harm (superficial wrist cutting) and intense, inconsolable emotional distress following an argument. Urgent after-hours assessment was mandated to assess the severity of the lacerations, ensure hemostasis, and provide acute behavioral de-escalation to prevent a fatal suicide attempt prior to routine hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "They left me, they always leave me. I had to cut to let the pressure out, I can't stand this emptiness."
History of Present Illness: The current acute presentation was triggered two hours ago by a minor disagreement with a romantic partner, which the patient interpreted as catastrophic rejection. This triggered frantic efforts to avoid real or imagined abandonment. The patient has a clear pattern of unstable and intense interpersonal relationships, rapidly alternating between extremes of idealization and devaluation. Following the argument, the patient experienced profound affective instability due to marked reactivity of mood, and chronic feelings of emptiness. This culminated in impulsivity and recurrent self-mutilating behavior (cutting the left forearm). The patient also displays identity disturbance and reports inappropriate, intense anger toward the partner, followed by transient, stress-related severe dissociative symptoms during the self-harm act.
Functional Status: Currently highly dysregulated, unable to maintain safety independently.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of recurrent, escalating self-harm; roommate is overwhelmed but supportive.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 | HR: 105 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Tearful, hyperventilating, holding a blood-stained towel to the left wrist, fluctuating between hostility and clinging dependency.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular and respiratory systems unremarkable. Integumentary exam reveals 4-5 superficial, parallel lacerations to the left volar forearm; bleeding is minimal and venous. No pulsatile bleeding, no underlying tendon or nerve involvement (full motor and sensory function intact in the hand). Mental state examination highlights an extreme fear of abandonment, severe emotional lability, and active engagement in recurrent self-harm or suicidality.
Environmental Observations: Razor blade safely secured by the roommate prior to arrival. Room is cluttered.
Medication Reconciliation: Prescribed Quetiapine 50mg PRN for distress; patient refused to take it prior to the self-harm.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Borderline personality disorder - Status: Exacerbated (Acute self-harm episode)
Differentials:

Bipolar Disorder - Ruled out/unlikely due to the mood shifts occurring over minutes to hours in direct response to interpersonal stressors, rather than sustained, autonomous mood episodes lasting days/weeks.

Major Depressive Disorder - Ruled out/unlikely due to the profound reactivity of mood; the patient's affect brightens rapidly when validated and supported, which is atypical for melancholic depression.

Arterial Laceration - Ruled out/unlikely due to the superficial nature of the wounds, absence of pulsatile bleeding, and intact distal perfusion and function.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, intentional self-harm and severe emotional dysregulation secondary to an interpersonal trigger. Telephone triage appropriately identified the urgent need for wound assessment and suicide risk stratification. Urgent after-hours medical intervention was essential to definitively clear the lacerations of neurovascular compromise, prevent infection, and establish a physical and psychological safety plan. Leaving the patient unassessed overnight carried an unacceptably high risk of the patient escalating the self-harm to a lethal level in an attempt to elicit care or numb the emotional pain.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Wounds cleaned, steri-stripped, and securely bandaged. Administered the patient's own prescribed Quetiapine 50mg PRN under observation to facilitate chemical de-escalation. Expected investigation results: psychological evaluation utilizing the MacLean Screening Instrument.
Positive Behavioral Change: Implement "TIPP" distress tolerance skills (Temperature, Intense exercise, Paced breathing, Paired muscle relaxation). Instruct the patient to immediately plunge their face into a bowl of ice water for 30 seconds when the urge to self-harm spikes. This triggers the mammalian dive reflex, rapidly downregulating the parasympathetic nervous system, providing immediate biological relief as a safe alternative to the relief sought through cutting.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to community Dialectical Behavior Therapy (DBT) program.
DME/Safety (Optional): Basic wound care supplies provided.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 48 hours for wound check and ongoing support.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the wounds begin to bleed continuously, if numbness in the hand develops, or if the patient forms an active plan with lethal intent to end their life.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Acute wound care and safety planning provided; BPD requires specialized, long-term psychotherapy (DBT).
Clinical Course: The acute emotional intensity will naturally burn out over the next 12-24 hours. The wounds will heal within a week if kept clean.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If the bleeding restarts and does not stop with firm pressure after 10 minutes, or if you feel completely unable to keep yourself safe from a suicide attempt tonight, you must go to the emergency department.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The physical wounds are shallow and safe. The intense emotional pain is a hallmark of your condition; it feels intolerable, but it will pass. The ice water trick safely mimics the physiological reset you get from cutting.
Actionable Steps: Keep the bandage dry. Use the ice-water facial plunge immediately if you feel the urge to cut again. Allow your roommate to hold all sharp objects tonight.

